detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
React is a component-based view library. You build small, self-contained pieces called components, each describing a part of the UI, and compose them into larger screens. A component is just a function that returns markup:
function Welcome() {
  return <h1>Hello</h1>;
}

JSX lets you write something like:
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello, {name}</h1>;
The curly braces let you drop any JavaScript expression into the markup. Browsers cannot read JSX directly, so a tool like Babel transforms it into plain JavaScript before it runs.

A component is a function whose job is to return a description of some UI. For example:
function Avatar({ src, name }) {
  return <img src={src} alt={name} />;
}
This Avatar component can be reused anywhere you need to show a user picture.

A functional component is a function:
function Hello({ name }) {
  return <p>Hi {name}</p>;
}
A class component does the same with more boilerplate:
class Hello extends React.Component {
  render() { return <p>Hi {this.props.name}</p>; }
}

Props are an object of values a parent passes to a child via JSX attributes. For example:
<Greeting name="Sam" />
Inside the child, you read it:
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello {name}</h1>;
}

State is a component's internal memory. In a functional component you create it with the useState Hook:
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
Here count is the current value and setCount updates it. Calling setCount triggers a re-render with the new value.

useState is the most basic React Hook for state. You use array destructuring to name the pair it returns:
const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
Here isOpen starts as false, and setIsOpen(true) updates it and triggers a re-render.

Conditional rendering is choosing what to display based on state or props. For example:
{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <LoginForm />}
or showing an element only when a condition holds:
{hasError && <ErrorBanner />}

Rendering a list looks like:
{users.map(user => <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>)}
The map produces an array of elements, and each one gets a key that uniquely identifies it among its siblings.

Default props are fallback values used when a parent does not pass a particular prop. In functional components you express them inline:
function Button({ variant = 'primary', disabled = false }) {
  return <button className={variant} disabled={disabled} />;
}
